01_07_01_00_06	Debug: {'system_prompt': '\n        You are a Fight The Landlord (Dou Dizhu) player. \n        ### Introduction\n        Fight the Landlord (Dou Dizhu) is a poker climbing game primarily for three players.\n        ### Players, Cards and Deal\n        This game uses a 54-card pack including two jokers, red and black. The cards rank from high to low:\n        red joker, black joker, 2, A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3. Suits are irrelevant.\n\n        ### Play\n        The landlord plays first, and may play a single card or any legal combination.\n        Each subsequent player in anticlockwise order must either pass (play no card) or beat the previous play\n        by playing a higher combination of the same number of cards and same type.\n        There are just two exceptions to this: a rocket can beat any combination, and a bomb can beat any combination\n        except a higher bomb or rocket.\n\n        ### Combinations\n        1. Single card: ranking from three (low) up to red joker (high).\n        2. Pair: two cards of the same rank, from three (low) up to two (high).\n        3. Triplet: three cards of the same rank.\n        4. Triplet with an attached card: a triplet with any single card added.\n        5. Triplet with an attached pair: a triplet with a pair added.\n        6. Sequence: at least five cards of consecutive rank, from 3 up to ace. Twos and jokers cannot be used.\n        7. Sequence of pairs: at least three pairs of consecutive ranks, from 3 up to ace. Twos and jokers cannot be used.\n        8. Sequence of triplets: at least two triplets of consecutive ranks from three up to ace.\n        9. Sequence of triplets with attached cards: an extra card is added to each triplet.\n        10. Sequence of triplets with attached pairs: an extra pair is attached to each triplet.\n        11. Bomb: four cards of the same rank. Beats everything except a rocket and a higher bomb.\n        12. Rocket: a pair of jokers. The highest combination, beats everything.\n        13. Quadplex set: a quad with two single cards OR two pairs attached.\n        Use the EXACT card symbols provided in the Valid Moves list.\n        Prepare a valid JSON object like {"cards": ["♠3", "♥3"]} for your answer, along with your explanation and reason. If you pass, output: {"cards": []}\n        \n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output the action with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n    ', 'user_prompt': "You are playing as a peasant. Your aim is to be the play out all your cards before the landlord does, OR help your teammate (the other peasant) play out their cards before the landlord does. If the landlord plays out all cards first, you lose.\nYou are a Peasant. The Previous Player (Left) is the Landlord (ENEMY). The Next Player (Right) is your TEAMMATE (Peasant). You must cooperate with your teammate to beat the Landlord. The objective is to empty your hand before the landlord does, or help your teammate empty their hand before the landlord does.\n\nYour Hand: ♣4 ♣5 ♠6 ♥6 ♣7 ♦8 ♠9 ♣9 ♠10 ♠J ♥J ♣Q ♦Q ♣K ♦A ♥2 RedJoker\nLast Play to Beat: ♦6\n--- Game History (Recent) ---\nTurn 1 (Prev Player): ♦6\n--- Player Info ---\nCards Held by Next Player (Right) [Peasant (Teammate)]: 17\nCards Held by Prev Player (Left) [Landlord (Opponent)]: 19\n--- Cards Remaining Unknown (Card Counting) ---\n(Format explanation: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nBlackJoker: 1, 2: 3, A: 3, K: 3, Q: 2, J: 2, 10: 3, 9: 2, 8: 3, 7: 3, 6: 1, 5: 3, 4: 3, 3: 4\nValid Moves:\nsingle: ♣7\nsingle: ♦8\nsingle: ♠9\nsingle: ♠10\nsingle: ♠J\nsingle: ♣Q\nsingle: ♣K\nsingle: ♦A\nsingle: ♥2\nsingle: RedJoker\npass: []\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '**The Game is Afoot: Strategic Analysis for Optimal Play**\n\nOkay, so here\'s the deal. I\'m playing Peasant, and it\'s my turn. The Landlord just tossed out a measly ♦6. Now, my immediate thought is to preserve my very strong hand with the goal of ultimately playing a massive straight (4-K) to clear my trash.\n\nHere\'s the breakdown of my thinking:\n\n1.  **Current Situation:** I\'m after the Landlord, and I need to do something.\n    My hand: ♣4 ♣5 ♠6 ♥6 ♣7 ♦8 ♠9 ♣9 ♠10 ♠J ♥J ♣Q ♦Q ♣K ♦A ♥2 RedJoker\n\n2.  **Hand Evaluation**: I\'ve got a killer hand with singles and pairs! I immediately see a HUGE potential straight, specifically 4-5-6-7-8-9-10-J-Q-K. To do so I need to preserve the following, the "straight" cards: 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, J, Q, K. If I clear these cards, I have a mess of pairs, plus singles A, 2 and RJ.\n\n3.  **The Goal:** My strategy here is simple: Get the lead and play a massive straight. With that said, I am going to make some calculations. I have to play a card to beat the 6. I\'d like to use as few cards as possible to force my opponent to put something down.\n\n4.  **Options Analysis:**\n    *   **Pass**: Passing might be okay on the surface. But since the 6 is so low, this move is too passive and might invite the landlord to continue playing low cards cheaply, which does not allow me to get rid of my huge hand. With a strong hand, I should attempt to disrupt the Landlord and maintain control or clear the hand.\n    *   **Single Card Plays**: I am evaluating all possible moves I can make. My initial thoughts are:\n        *   Playing an 7, 8, 10, K is a terrible idea since those cards are essential to the straight.\n        *   Playing a 6: Illegal. Previous move was a 6.\n        *   A - Good. But with A, 2, and RJ, I don\'t want to use A.\n        *   Playing any of the 9, J, Q from pairs, is a good strategic play to disrupt the opponent and clear a "spare" card. And 9 is the cheapest.\n\n5.  **Decision Time:** Here\'s the most critical point: I need to decide if I play A to secure the turn and take a fast-track, OR do I play a low spare? And so:\n    *   I realize that even though I have two top-tier cards, I have to clear the singles. I am going to have to make 4 turns to get rid of the singles, so I should save the heavy cards to make sure I am able to clear. This means playing a low card.\n    *   My objective here is to maintain a dominant hand position, and the best way to do so is playing 9 to clear the card, and give me a clear hand for the straight. Playing 9 is a super smart move, as this forces the hand position.\n    *   If I play 9, I get rid of an "extra" card, force someone to have to respond and preserve my 4-K straight for later.\n\n6.  **Strategic Implications:**\n    *   Now, I have evaluated all possible combinations to achieve hand clearance. I should keep the 4-K to make sure I get a good hand clear.\n    *   I need to balance getting the lead, playing the 4-K, and clearing my garbage singletons. Playing 9 is the way to do so.\n\n7.  **Final Verdict:** I have made my move, and I am going to play ♠9. I believe this is a strong strategic play. I am getting rid of the spare, and keeping the big cards to maintain control.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The hand contains a very strong 10-card straight (4-5-6-7-8-9-10-J-Q-K) and high control cards (A, 2, Red Joker). The remaining cards after forming the straight are duplicates/spares (6, 9, J, Q). To win, I need to clear these spare singles. I have 3 control cards (A, 2, RJ) which can ideally cover 3 spare singles. Currently, I have 4 spare singles (6, 9, J, Q). Beating the current play (6) with one of these spares is crucial to balance the hand.\n\nReason: Playing ♠9 is the most strategic move. It beats the Landlord\'s ♦6 cheaply while clearing a "spare" card (since I have a pair of 9s, one is used for the straight 4-K, the other is extra). This leaves me with 3 spare singles (6, J, Q) and 3 control cards (A, 2, Red Joker) to regain the lead, creating a perfect 1:1 ratio to clear my hand. Playing a higher card like ♦A would waste a control card on a weak lead, leaving me with too many trash cards and not enough entries. Playing 7 or 8 would break the 10-card straight.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠9"]}'}
